body:
## Motivation

The current JMH suites isolate library operations well, but their fixtures are intentionally generic. With three active consumers, we need additional reproducible workload shapes so an optimization that is excellent for one application is not promoted as a universal default.

These remain **library benchmarks**, not full application benchmarks. Fixtures must be synthetic, deterministic and free of consumer data while reflecting the operation mix and resource pressure each consumer creates.

## Profile A — audio-analyzer

Model a process in which Git/database persistence competes with CPU- and memory-intensive audio work:

- medium and large non-compressible binary/object publications;
- metadata commits that reference results without sending audio payloads into Hibernate Search;
- repeated readback of recent artifacts and refs;
- configurable background CPU/memory pressure representing analysis;
- single and concurrent repositories;
- stateful/stateless writer selection, chunk sizing and bounded memory;
- allocation, GC, transaction, JDBC batch and database-log evidence.

Questions:

- Does persistence stay bounded while analysis occupies most CPU/memory?
- Do optional Search changes leak cost into a Core-only workload?
- At which object/pack size do chunk and writer defaults change?

## Profile B — Taxonomy

Model many repositories/workspaces with predominantly small textual/structured commits:

- 1k/10k/100k commits distributed over multiple logical repositories;
- branches, merges, reflogs and concurrent workspace publication;
- author/committer/time/path/full-text queries representative of requirement/taxonomy history;
- compact hit projection versus entity hydration;
- incremental indexing, interrupted rebuild, purge and restart;
- PostgreSQL with local and realistic network RTT;
- search result to authoritative Git object/ref verification.

Questions:

- Where do SQL path queries cross over with Lucene path queries?
- How do multi-repository filters and reflog indexes scale?
- What batch, refresh and purge sizes remain bounded for interactive use?

## Profile C — sandbox

Model Java-tooling histories rather than generic natural-language text:

- Java source trees with repeated cleanup/refactoring commits;
- package/class/method rename and move patterns;
- path and Java-analysis queries used by cleanup tooling;
- incremental update after a small source edit versus full analysis rebuild;
- memory footprint suitable for an Eclipse process;
- Java Analysis enabled without unrelated server/application dependencies.

Questions:

- Which index/content profile is useful for Java tooling?
- Does incremental semantic analysis avoid full-history rescans?
- What is the runtime and index-size cost inside an IDE-sized memory budget?

## Shared benchmark requirements

- preserve stable operation names and chart anchors;
- tag series with consumer relevance as metadata, without duplicating history;
- retain raw JMH JSON, allocation/GC, ORM/JDBC and database-native evidence where applicable;
- define cold/warm cache and local/remote database conditions explicitly;
- include the current release, current main and each proposed implementation in comparisons;
- document theoretical lower bounds such as required payload bytes, transactions and network round trips;
- keep smoke profiles suitable for pull requests and larger profiles manual/scheduled;
- never change a production default solely from one consumer profile.

## Acceptance criteria

- all three deterministic fixture generators are committed and tested;
- each profile has a bounded smoke matrix plus a larger scheduled/manual matrix;
- the dashboard clearly distinguishes generic and consumer-shaped fixtures;
- consumer compatibility contracts pass for the same commit whose benchmark data is published;
- documentation states where results do and do not generalize;
- at least one real optimization decision is made from each profile rather than adding charts without an operational consequence.
